{"question": "A 34-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department following a high-speed motor vehicle collision in which she was the restrained driver. On arrival, she has a cervical collar in place and is unresponsive. Her temperature is 36.7\u00b0C (98\u00b0F), pulse is 70/min, and blood pressure is 131/76 mm Hg. Her pupils are dilated and not reactive to light. She is unresponsive to noxious stimuli. She is intubated, and mechanical ventilation is begun. Serum concentrations of electrolytes, urea nitrogen, and creatinine are within the reference range. Toxicology screening is negative. A CT scan of the head without contrast shows a massive intracranial bleed and a cervical spine injury at the level of C3. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosing brain death?", "options": {"A": "Electroencephalography", "B": "Magnetic resonance angiography", "C": "Deep tendon reflex testing", "D": "Transcranial Doppler ultrasonography", "E": "Corneal reflex test"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Neurosurgery", "Neurology", "Traumatology", "Intensive Care Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Critical Care Medicine"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The scenario describes a 34-year-old woman who has been involved in a high-speed motor vehicle collision as the restrained driver. She is currently unresponsive, intubated, and has a cervical collar in place due to suspected cervical spine injury. Her vital signs are stable, but her pupils are dilated and non-reactive to light, indicating a severe neurological injury. The CT scan of the head reveals a massive intracranial bleed, and there is a cervical spine injury at the C3 level. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Severe Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI):** The presence of a massive intracranial bleed and unresponsiveness to noxious stimuli strongly suggest a severe TBI. 2. **Cervical Spine Injury:** The cervical spine inj